1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,140
The campaign against
Poland is still seen today

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:05,710
as the first Blitzkrieg in history.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,120
Yet the word appeared in
no German army manual,

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:13,390
and the Polish army fought
longer than many had expected.

5
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,370
Even so, after five weeks Poland
had vanished from the map,

6
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:20,160
divided between two dictatorships.

7
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:24,770
And behind the front, a war against the
civilian population had already begun.

8
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:33,140
On the morning of 1 September 1939,

9
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:38,170
about one and a half million German
soldiers stood on the Polish border.

10
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,090
They attacked from three
directions at once:

11
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,680
from Pomerania and East
Prussia in the north,

12
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,870
from Silesia in the southwest
and from Slovakia in the south.

13
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:50,750
Poland was surrounded on three sides.

14
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:58,120
The Wehrmacht had more than 2,500 tanks,

15
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:02,970
most of them light models,
and more than 2,000 aircraft.

16
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:06,370
Poland had just under a
million men under arms,

17
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:11,010
a few hundred tanks and about 400
combat aircraft ready for action.

18
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,490
Many reservists were still
on their way to their units.

19
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:17,140
At the urging of Britain and France,

20
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:20,010
Warsaw had postponed general mobilisation

21
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:22,150
so as not to give Hitler a pretext.

22
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:26,800
Only the small Polish navy had
brought some of its ships to safety.

23
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:32,840
Two days before the war began, the
destroyers Burza, Błyskawica and Grom

24
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:36,310
left the Baltic and reached Britain.

25
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:40,050
From there they fought on
alongside the Royal Navy.

26
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:46,880
In the very minutes

27
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:51,630
when the battleship Schleswig-Holstein
opened fire on the Westerplatte,

28
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,190
German dive bombers dropped
their bombs on Wieluń,

29
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:57,970
a small town of no military importance.

30
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,230
The hospital was hit as well.

31
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:02,410
Hundreds of residents died,

32
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:05,350
and most of the town centre lay in ruins.

33
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:10,390
In Danzig, Polish postal workers
defended their post office for hours.

34
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:14,410
Only when the building was set
on fire did they surrender.

35
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:17,300
Most of the survivors
were, a few weeks later,

36
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:21,530
sentenced to death by a
German court martial and shot.

37
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,460
On the Westerplatte,
about 200 Polish soldiers

38
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:28,290
under Major Henryk Sucharski held out

39
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:32,130
against naval guns, dive
bombers and assault troops.

40
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:35,390
The plan was for them to
hold out for twelve hours.

41
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:37,690
It became seven days.

42
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:45,640
On the evening of the first day of
the war, near Krojanty, Polish uhlans

43
00:02:45,660 --> 00:02:50,450
charged a German infantry unit with
drawn sabres and drove it back.

44
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:54,600
Then German armoured cars
appeared and opened fire.

45
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:59,510
Italian war correspondents who visited
the battlefield shortly afterwards

46
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:04,270
turned it into a story of horsemen
charging tanks with lances.

47
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:07,190
German propaganda spread it further.

48
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:11,190
The story is still well
known today, but it is false.

49
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:15,020
Polish cavalry mostly fought
dismounted, like infantry,

50
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:17,930
and had its own anti-tank guns.

51
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:24,420
Two days later, in
Bromberg, shooting broke out

52
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:28,570
between Polish soldiers and
members of the German minority.

53
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:31,450
More than a hundred ethnic
Germans were killed.

54
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,870
How it happened and exactly how
many died is still disputed today.

55
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:41,810
Nazi propaganda called it
Bromberg Bloody Sunday,

56
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:46,730
multiplied the number of victims and
used it to justify mass shootings.

57
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:51,140
After the town was taken, hundreds
of Polish residents were executed.

58
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:56,590
Behind the troops came Einsatzgruppen
of the Security Police and the SD.

59
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,630
They carried lists that named teachers,

60
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:03,560
priests, doctors,
officials and landowners.

61
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,400
By the end of the year,
German units had murdered

62
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:10,950
tens of thousands of Polish
civilians, many of them Jews.

63
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:21,040
At the front, the German armoured and
motorised divisions advanced quickly.

64
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:25,160
The Luftwaffe destroyed airfields,
bridges and railway lines

65
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:29,490
and attacked columns on the
roads, refugee columns too.

66
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:34,330
After one week, the first German
tanks reached the outskirts of Warsaw.

67
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:38,950
A first attack on the city failed
at the defenders' barricades.

68
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:43,400
West of the capital, two Polish
armies had meanwhile gathered

69
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:45,140
after falling back from the border.

70
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:48,300
On 9 September, on the Bzura,

71
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:51,170
they attacked the flank
of the German 8th Army.

72
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:54,350
It became the largest
battle of the campaign.

73
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:58,190
For several days German
divisions were hard pressed,

74
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,250
then the Polish forces were encircled.

75
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:04,450
Tens of thousands of
soldiers were taken prisoner.

76
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:11,780
While the army fought, Poland
saved its national treasure.

77
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:15,560
In the very first week of
the war, lorries and buses

78
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:20,330
carrying about 75 tonnes of the Polish
national bank's gold left the capital.

79
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:25,090
On roads full of refugees, the
convoy reached the Romanian border,

80
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:28,100
from there the gold went
by train to the Black Sea

81
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:31,490
and by ship via Turkey to France.

82
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:34,270
The Germans never got their hands on it.

83
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:37,180
Poland waited for help from the West.

84
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,760
France had promised, no later
than two weeks after mobilisation,

85
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:44,970
to launch a major
offensive against Germany.

86
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:49,810
In fact, French troops advanced
into the Saarland on 7 September,

87
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:52,830
a few kilometres, through
evacuated villages.

88
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:57,330
Then they stopped, and in
October they pulled back again.

89
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:01,510
British bombers dropped
mainly leaflets over Germany.

90
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:06,540
The French called this time the
drôle de guerre, the strange war.

91
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,630
In Germany it was called the Sitzkrieg.

92
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:14,940
Early on the morning of 17 September,

93
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:18,230
the Red Army crossed
Poland's eastern border.

94
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:22,180
Moscow declared that the
Polish state no longer existed

95
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:25,530
and that it was coming to protect
Ukrainians and Belarusians.

96
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:27,520
In reality, the Soviet
Union was carrying out

97
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:31,610
the secret protocol of
the Hitler-Stalin Pact.

98
00:06:31,880 --> 00:06:34,260
There were hardly any Polish
troops left in the east.

99
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:37,840
The following night, the Polish
government left the country

100
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:39,690
across the Romanian border.

101
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:45,130
A few days later, the Wehrmacht handed the
city of Brest-Litovsk over to the Red Army.

102
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:50,260
General Heinz Guderian and the Soviet
brigade commander Semyon Krivoshein

103
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:53,490
reviewed the march past
of their troops together.

104
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:01,510
On the Baltic, the Hel peninsula held out,

105
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:04,420
a narrow strip of land across from Gdynia,

106
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:08,860
where about 2,800 Polish
soldiers and sailors

107
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:12,530
had dug in under Rear Admiral Józef Unrug.

108
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:16,340
And further north, a Polish
submarine made an escape

109
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:18,130
that is still told today.

110
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:21,980
The Orzeł had put into the
neutral port of Tallinn

111
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,010
and been interned there.

112
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:28,190
The Estonians took away her
charts and part of her ammunition.

113
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:32,440
On the night of 18 September, the
crew broke out of the harbour.

114
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:37,050
With no charts, only a
hand-drawn list of lighthouses,

115
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:40,220
she crept for weeks through
the Baltic and the straits

116
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,010
and reached Scotland in mid-October.

117
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:52,710
Warsaw was now shelled
and bombed day and night.

118
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:57,530
Waterworks and power stations
failed, whole streets burned.

119
00:07:57,720 --> 00:08:02,580
Every evening, Mayor Stefan Starzyński
spoke to the residents on the radio

120
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,160
and urged them to hold out.

121
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:07,450
After the surrender he stayed in the city.

122
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:12,150
The Germans arrested him, and
he later died in German custody.

123
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:17,740
On 25 September, the city suffered
the heaviest air raid of the siege.

124
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:20,910
Two days later the city
asked for a ceasefire,

125
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:25,270
and on 28 September the
surrender was signed.

126
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:29,670
More than 20,000 civilians
had died in Warsaw,

127
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:33,360
about 140,000 soldiers
were taken prisoner.

128
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:37,010
The fortress of Modlin
surrendered a day later,

129
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:39,610
the Hel peninsula in early October.

130
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:43,250
The last fighting ended
on 6 October near Kock.

131
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:47,770
The day before, Hitler had taken
a victory parade in Warsaw.

132
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,550
But Poland had not surrendered.

133
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:58,730
In Paris, a government in exile formed
under General Władysław Sikorski.

134
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:01,480
Tens of thousands of
Polish soldiers and pilots

135
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:04,040
had escaped through Romania and Hungary

136
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:07,830
and made their way to France to fight on.

137
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:11,590
About 66,000 Polish
soldiers had been killed,

138
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:14,710
the Wehrmacht had lost about 16,000 men.

139
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:19,550
Hundreds of thousands of Poles were taken
prisoner by the Germans or the Soviets.

140
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,493
On the very day Warsaw surrendered,

141
00:09:21,513 --> 00:09:26,510
Ribbentrop and Molotov signed
another treaty in Moscow.

142
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:29,950
The new border largely
followed the river Bug,

143
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:33,510
and in return Lithuania fell into
the Soviet sphere of interest.

144
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:40,690
Germany annexed western Poland
directly into the Reich.

145
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:44,060
The rest of the occupied territory
became the General Government,

146
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:47,610
headed by Hans Frank,
with its seat in Kraków.

147
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:51,830
There, the Polish population
was to serve only as labour.

148
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,450
Universities and secondary
schools were closed.

149
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:57,440
In November, in Kraków,
the Gestapo arrested

150
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:01,160
more than 180 professors
and staff of the university

151
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:04,520
and sent them to Sachsenhausen
concentration camp.

152
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:09,870
In Piotrków, the first ghetto in occupied
Poland had already been set up in October.

153
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:14,670
In the Soviet-occupied east, too,
arrests and deportations began.

154
00:10:14,740 --> 00:10:18,640
The following spring, the
Soviet secret police, the NKVD,

155
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:24,070
shot about 22,000 Polish officers
at Katyn and other sites,

156
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:27,150
along with policemen and
members of the intelligentsia.

157
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:34,200
For Poland, an occupation began
that would last almost six years.

158
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:38,950
About six million Polish
citizens did not survive the war,

159
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:41,520
about half of them Jews.

160
00:10:41,540 --> 00:10:45,070
But Poland had already passed
something on before the war.

161
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:49,060
In July 1939, Polish mathematicians

162
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:51,870
had shown British and French cryptologists

163
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:55,850
how the German Enigma cipher
machine could be broken.

164
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:57,990
The Wehrmacht had no idea.

165
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:01,180
In the West, a deceptive calm now reigned.

166
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:04,390
But Hitler was already
planning the next attack.
